add_update_server() reuses a server entry that NetworkManager's D-Bus
update (or a new resolv.conf) left marked, matching it only by domain, and
gives it the new server's address and interface name but leaves the
interface index cached for the old one. With --bind-interfaces (as
NetworkManager runs it) that index is only refreshed by a TCP query, and
local_bind() sends with IP_UNICAST_IF on it instead of SO_BINDTODEVICE by
name. NetworkManager makes a WireGuard connection's interface anew, with a
new index, each time it connects, so once a TCP query has cached the
tunnel's index, every query after the next switch fails with "failed to
bind server socket to <interface>: Address not available": through the
tunnel nothing resolves. Without an interface, the old one's name was
kept too. Reset both: an index of 0 binds by name, as a new entry does.

--- a/src/domain-match.c
+++ b/src/domain-match.c
@@ -759,8 +759,14 @@
       serv->uid = rand32();
 #endif      
 	  
+      /* The entry reused above may have been another server's: keep
+	 neither its interface nor the index cached for it, which may
+	 be of an interface since deleted (a VPN's). */
       if (interface)
 	safe_strncpy(serv->interface, interface, sizeof(serv->interface));
+      else
+	serv->interface[0] = 0;
+      serv->ifindex = 0;
       if (addr)
 	serv->addr = *addr;
       if (source_addr)
